1
00:00:03,599 --> 00:00:07,957
TANTRUM She had got up very early in the morning and had worked hard in the

2
00:00:07,957 --> 00:00:13,439
garden and she was tired and sleepy, so as soon as Martha had brought her supper

3
00:00:13,439 --> 00:00:16,957
and she had eaten it, she was glad to go to

4
00:00:16,957 --> 00:00:21,200
bed. As she laid her head on the pillow she murmured to

5
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:26,159
herself: "I'll go out before breakfast and work with Dicken and then afterward— —I

6
00:00:26,159 --> 00:00:31,319
believe— —I'll go to see him." She thought it was the middle of the

7
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:37,558
night when she was wake by such dreadful sounds that she jumped out of bed in an

8
00:00:37,558 --> 00:00:45,319
instant. What was it— —what was it? The next minute she felt quite sure she knew.

9
00:00:45,319 --> 00:00:49,957
Doors were opened and shut and there were hurrying feet in the corridors and

10
00:00:49,957 --> 00:00:54,680
someone was crying and screaming at the same time, screaming and crying in a

11
00:00:54,680 --> 00:01:01,080
horrible way. "It's Colin," she said. "He's having one of those tantrums the nurse

12
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:07,637
called hysterics. How awful it sounds." As she listened to the sobbing

13
00:01:07,637 --> 00:01:11,520
screams she did not wonder that people were so frightened that they gave him

14
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,119
his own way in everything rather than hear them. She put her hands over her

15
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:22,319
ears and felt sick and shivering. "I don't know what to do. I

16
00:01:22,319 --> 00:01:28,360
don't know what to do," she kept saying. "I can't bear

17
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:34,797
it." Once she wondered if he would stop if she dared go to him and then she

18
00:01:34,797 --> 00:01:38,637
remembered how he had driven her out of the room and thought that perhaps the

19
00:01:38,637 --> 00:01:43,319
sight of her might make him worse. Even when she pressed her hands

20
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:49,759
more tightly over her ears she could not keep the awful sounds out. She hated them

21
00:01:49,759 --> 00:01:55,240
so and was so terrified by them that suddenly they began to make her angry

22
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:59,400
and she felt as if she should like to fly into a tantrum herself and frighten

23
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:05,479
him as he was frightening her. She was was not used to anyone's tempers but her

24
00:02:05,479 --> 00:02:11,400
own. She took her hands from her ears and sprang up and stamped her foot. "He ought

25
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:18,159
to be stopped! Somebody ought to make him stop! Somebody ought to beat him!" she cried

26
00:02:18,159 --> 00:02:23,718
out. Just then she heard feet almost running down the corridor and her door

27
00:02:23,718 --> 00:02:31,240
opened and the nurse came in. She was not laughing now by any means. She even looked rather

28
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:37,360
pale. "He's worked himself into hysterics," she said in a great hurry. "He'll do

29
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:44,200
himself harm. No one can do anything with him. You come and try, like a good child.

30
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:52,119
He likes you." "He turned me out of the room this morning," said Mary, stamping her foot with

31
00:02:52,119 --> 00:02:57,919
excitement. The stamp rather pleased the nurse. The truth was that she had been

32
00:02:57,919 --> 00:03:03,439
afraid she might find Mary crying and hiding her head under the bed clothes

33
00:03:03,439 --> 00:03:09,439
That's right," she said. "You're in the right humor. You go and scold him. Give

34
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:16,119
him something new to think of. Do go, child, as quick as ever you can." It was

35
00:03:16,119 --> 00:03:20,957
not until afterward that Mary realized that the thing had been funny as well as

36
00:03:20,957 --> 00:03:25,319
dreadful— —that it was funny that all the grown-up people were so frightened that

37
00:03:25,319 --> 00:03:29,439
they came to a little girl just because they guessed she was almost as bad as

38
00:03:29,439 --> 00:03:34,200
Colin himself. She flew along the corridor coridor and the nearer she got

39
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:40,040
to the screams the higher her temper mounted. She felt quite wicked by the

40
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:45,558
time she reached the door. She slapped it open with her hand and ran across the

41
00:03:45,558 --> 00:03:53,119
room to the for posted bed. "You stop!" she almost shouted. "You stop! I hate you!

42
00:03:53,119 --> 00:03:57,957
Everybody hates you! I wish everybody would run out of the house and let you

43
00:03:57,957 --> 00:04:04,319
scream yourself to death! You will scream yourself to death in a minute, and I wish you

44
00:04:04,319 --> 00:04:10,797
would!" A nice sympathetic child could neither have thought nor said such

45
00:04:10,797 --> 00:04:16,599
things, but it just happened that the shock of hearing them was the best

46
00:04:16,599 --> 00:04:23,759
possible thing for this hysterical boy whom no one had ever dared to restrain or

47
00:04:23,759 --> 00:04:29,797
contradict. He had been lying on his face beating his pillow with his hands and he

48
00:04:29,797 --> 00:04:33,479
actually almost jumped around, he turned so quick Qui ly at the sound of the

49
00:04:33,479 --> 00:04:39,680
furious little voice. His face looked dreadful, white and red and swollen, and

50
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:46,680
he was gasping and choking; but savage little Mary did not care an

51
00:04:46,878 --> 00:04:52,680
atom. "If you scream another scream," she said, "I'll scream too— —and I can scream

52
00:04:52,680 --> 00:05:00,000
louder than you can and I'll frighten you, I'll frighten you!" He actually had stopped screaming

53
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,319
because she had startled him so. The scream which had been coming almost

54
00:05:04,319 --> 00:05:09,878
choked him. The tears were streaming down his face and he shook all

55
00:05:09,878 --> 00:05:16,479
over. "I can't stop!" he gasped and sobbed. "I can't— —I

56
00:05:16,479 --> 00:05:24,200
can't!" "You can!" shouted Mary. "Half that ails you is hysterics and temper— —just

57
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:31,080
hysterics— —hysterics— —hysterics!" and she stamped each time she said it. "I felt the

58
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:35,360
lump— —I felt it," choked out Colin. "I knew I

59
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:40,360
should. I shall have a hunch on my back and then I shall die," and he began to

60
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:46,718
rthe again and turned on his face and sobbed and wailed but he didn't

61
00:05:46,718 --> 00:05:49,599
scream. "You didn't feel a lump!"

62
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:55,119
contradicted Mary fiercely. "If you did it was only a hysterical lump. Hysterics

63
00:05:55,119 --> 00:05:59,360
makes lumps. There's nothing the matter with your horrid back— —nothing but

64
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:03,159
hysterics! Turn over and let me look at it!"

65
00:06:03,159 --> 00:06:08,240
She liked the word "hysterics" and felt somehow as if it had an effect on him. He

66
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:15,878
was probably like herself and had never heard it before. "Nurse," she commanded, "come here and

67
00:06:15,878 --> 00:06:21,240
show me his back this minute!" The nurse, Mrs. Medlock and Martha

68
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:26,637
had been standing huddled together near the door staring at her, their mouths half

69
00:06:26,637 --> 00:06:32,558
open. All three had gasped with fright more than once. The nurse came forward as

70
00:06:32,558 --> 00:06:38,040
if she were half afraid. Colin was heaving with great breathless sobs.

71
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:45,400
"Perhaps he— —he won't let me," she hesitated in a low voice. Colin heard her, however,

72
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:53,919
and he gasped out between two sobs: "Sh— —show her! She— —she'll see

73
00:06:53,919 --> 00:06:59,520
then!" It was a poor thin back to look at when it was Beed Every rib could be

74
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:04,599
counted and every joint of the spine, though Mistress Mary did not count them

75
00:07:04,599 --> 00:07:11,279
as she bent over and examined them with a solemn savage little face. She looked so sour and

76
00:07:11,279 --> 00:07:16,919
old-fashioned that the nurse turned her head aside to hide the twitching of her

77
00:07:16,919 --> 00:07:22,919
mouth. There was just a minute's silence, for even Colin tried to hold his

78
00:07:22,919 --> 00:07:29,319
breath while Mary looked up and down his spine, and down and up, as intently as if

79
00:07:29,319 --> 00:07:35,840
she had been the great doctor from London. "There's not a single lump there!"

80
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:42,080
she said at last. "There's not a lump as big as a pin— —except backbone lumps, and

81
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:47,080
you can only feel them because you're thin. I've got backbone lumps myself, and

82
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:49,279
they used to stick out as much as yours

83
00:07:49,279 --> 00:07:54,599
do, until I began to get fatter, and I am not fat enough yet to hide them. There's

84
00:07:54,599 --> 00:07:59,159
not a lump as big as a pin! If you ever say there is again, I shall

85
00:07:59,159 --> 00:08:04,319
laugh!" No one but Colin himself knew what a effect those crossly spoken childish

86
00:08:04,319 --> 00:08:08,637
words had on him. If he had ever had anyone to talk to about his secret

87
00:08:08,637 --> 00:08:14,040
terrors— —if he had ever dared to let himself ask questions— —if he had had

88
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:19,200
childish companions and had not lay on his back in the huge closed house,

89
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:23,119
breathing an atmosphere heavy with the fears of people who were most of them

90
00:08:23,119 --> 00:08:27,919
ignorant and tired of him, he would have found out that most of his fright and

91
00:08:27,919 --> 00:08:35,957
illness was created by himself. But he had lain and thought of himself and his aches and

92
00:08:35,957 --> 00:08:42,878
weariness for hours and days and months and years. And now that an angry

93
00:08:42,878 --> 00:08:48,240
unsympathetic little girl insisted obstinately that he was not as ill as he

94
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:53,080
thought he was he actually felt as if she might be speaking the

95
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:58,840
truth. "I didn't know," ventured the nurse, "that he thought he had a lump on his

96
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:04,439
spine. His back is weak because he won't try to sit up. I could have told him

97
00:09:04,439 --> 00:09:10,479
there was no lump there." Colin gulped and turned his face a little to look at

98
00:09:10,479 --> 00:09:23,000
her could could you?" he said pathetically. "Yes, sir." "There!" said Mary, and she gulped too.

99
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:28,319
Colin turned on his face again and but for his long drawn broken breaths, which

100
00:09:28,319 --> 00:09:33,840
were the dying down of his storm of sobbing, he lay still for a minute, though

101
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:39,718
great tears streamed down his face and wet the pillow. Actually the tears meant

102
00:09:39,718 --> 00:09:45,718
that a curious great relief had come to him. Presently he turned and looked at

103
00:09:45,718 --> 00:09:50,957
the nurse again and strangely enough he was not like a rajer at all as he spoke

104
00:09:50,957 --> 00:10:00,240
to her. "Do you think— —I could— —live to grow up?" he said. The nurse was neither clever

105
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:05,919
nor soft-hearted but she could repeat some of the London doctor's words. "You

106
00:10:05,919 --> 00:10:10,159
probably will if you will do what you are told to do and not give way to your

107
00:10:10,159 --> 00:10:16,119
temper, and stay out a great deal in the fresh air." Colin's tantrum had passed and

108
00:10:16,119 --> 00:10:22,080
he was weak and worn out with crying and this perhaps made him feel gentle. He put

109
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:26,840
out his hand a little toward Mary, and I am glad to say that, her own tantrum

110
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:31,878
having passed, she was softened too and met him halfway with her hand, so that it

111
00:10:31,878 --> 00:10:37,957
was was a sort of making up. "I'll— —I'll go out with you, Mary," he

112
00:10:37,957 --> 00:10:44,637
said. "I shun hate fresh air if we can find—" He remembered just in time to stop

113
00:10:44,637 --> 00:10:51,080
himself from saying "if we can find the secret garden" and he ended, "I shall like

114
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:53,520
to go out with you if Dickon will come

115
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:58,919
and push my chair. I do so want to see Dickon and the fox and the

116
00:10:58,919 --> 00:11:05,279
crow." The nurse remade the tumbled bed and shook and straightened the pillows.

117
00:11:05,279 --> 00:11:11,000
Then she made Colin a cup of beef tea and gave a cup to Mary, who really was

118
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:16,279
very glad to get it after her excitement. Mrs. Medlock and Martha gladly

119
00:11:16,279 --> 00:11:21,200
slipped away, and after everything was neat and calm and in order the nurse

120
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:26,479
looked as if she would very gladly slip away also. She was a healthy young woman

121
00:11:26,479 --> 00:11:32,840
who resented being robbed of her sleep and she yawned quite openly as she looked at Mary,

122
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:40,319
who had pushed her big foot stol close to the four-posted bed and was holding Colin's

123
00:11:40,319 --> 00:11:46,439
hand. "You must go back and get your sleep out," she said. "He'll drop off after a

124
00:11:46,439 --> 00:11:51,680
while— —if he's not too upset. Then I'll lie down myself in the next

125
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:56,637
room." "Would you like me to sing you that song I learned from May Aya Mary

126
00:11:56,637 --> 00:12:03,319
whispered to Colin. His hand pulled hers gently and he turned his tired ey eyes on her

127
00:12:03,319 --> 00:12:10,520
appealingly. "Oh, yes!" he answered. "It's such a soft song. I shall go to sleep in a

128
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:16,680
minute." "I will put him to sleep," Mary said to the yawning nurse. "You can go if you

129
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:23,000
like." "Well," said the nurse, with an attempt at reluctance. "If he doesn't go to sleep

130
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,200
in half an hour you must call me." "Very well," answered

131
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:30,759
Mary. The nurse was out of the room in a

132
00:12:30,759 --> 00:12:34,919
minute and as soon soon as she was gone Colin pulled Mary's hand

133
00:12:34,919 --> 00:12:42,200
again. "I almost told," he said; "but I stopped myself in time. I won't talk and

134
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,119
I'll go to sleep, but you said you had a

135
00:12:45,119 --> 00:12:50,400
whole lot of nice things to tell me. Have you— —do you think you have found out

136
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:55,718
anything at all about the way into the secret garden?" Mary looked at his poor little

137
00:12:55,718 --> 00:13:04,080
tired face and swollen eyes and her heart relented yes yes she answered, "I think I

138
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:11,479
have. And if you will go to sleep I will tell you tomorrow His hand quite

139
00:13:11,479 --> 00:13:19,040
trembled. "Oh, Mary!" he said. "Oh, Mary! If I could get into it I think I should live

140
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:24,400
to grow up! Do you suppose that instead of singing the a song— —you could just

141
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:28,360
tell me softly as you did that first day what you imagine it looks like

142
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:32,558
inside? I am sure it will make me go to sleep."

143
00:13:32,558 --> 00:13:39,680
"Yes," answered Mary. "Shut your eyes." He closed his eyes and lay quite

144
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:45,878
still and she held his hand and began to speak very slowly and in a very low

145
00:13:45,878 --> 00:13:51,479
voice. "I think it has been left alone so long— —that it has grown all into a lovely

146
00:13:51,479 --> 00:13:57,400
tangle. I think the roses have climbed and climbed and climbed until they hang

147
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:04,040
from the branches and walls and creep over the ground— —almost like a strange gray

148
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:10,159
mist. Some of them have died but many— —are alive and when the summer comes there

149
00:14:10,159 --> 00:14:15,759
will be curtains and fountains of roses. I think the ground is full of

150
00:14:15,759 --> 00:14:21,439
daffodils and snow drops and lies and iris working their way out of the dark.

151
00:14:21,439 --> 00:14:28,240
Now the spring has begun— —perhaps— —perhaps—" The soft drone of her voice was

152
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:32,957
making him stiller and stiller and she saw it and went on.

153
00:14:32,957 --> 00:14:37,240
"Perhaps they are coming up through the grass— —perhaps there are clusters of

154
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:41,558
purple crocuses and gold ones— —even now.

155
00:14:41,558 --> 00:14:46,759
Perhaps the leaves are beginning to break out and uncurl— —and perhaps— —the

156
00:14:46,759 --> 00:14:53,558
gray is changing and a green GW veil is creeping— —and creeping over—

157
00:14:53,558 --> 00:14:59,159
—everything. And the birds are coming to look at it— —because it is— —so safe and

158
00:14:59,159 --> 00:15:07,360
still. And perhaps perhap perhaps— —perhaps—" very softly and slowly indeed,

159
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:15,159
"the robin has found a mate— —and is building a nest." And Colin was

160
00:15:15,919 --> 00:15:16,000
asleep. CHAPTER
